Date: November 27, 2010

Location: Mandalay Bay Events Center, Las Vegas (on HBO)

Result: Jason Litzau def. Celestino Caballero by Split Decision (10 rounds)

Scores: 96-94 Litzau, 97-93 Litzau, 97-93 Caballero

Significance: One of the biggest upsets of 2010 (Ring Magazine Upset of the Year). Litzau was a heavy underdog (around 13-1 or worse).
Fighter Backgrounds

Celestino Caballero ("The Panamanian Express"): Dominant southpaw champion. Tall (5'11"+ with reach advantage), skilled, high-output pressure fighter with excellent defense and boxing IQ. Entering the fight with strong momentum and considered one of the best in the division.
Jason Litzau ("The American Boy"): Aggressive, exciting orthodox boxer-puncher from St. Paul, Minnesota. Known for volume, power (especially inside), and willingness to trade. Came in with a solid but not elite-level record at the time.

Fight Breakdown
Style Clash: Classic pressure vs. slick southpaw. Caballero had the height/reach advantage and wanted to control distance with jabs and combinations. Litzau needed to close the gap, work the body, and make it a phone-booth fight.
Key Moments & Strategy:

Early Rounds: Caballero started well, using his jab and movement. Litzau was the busier fighter though, pressing forward and landing solid shots.
Mid-Fight (Rounds 4-7): Litzau’s aggression paid off. He was effective inside, landing power shots that visibly affected Caballero. CompuBox stats showed Litzau out-landed Caballero overall in several key categories despite Caballero landing slightly more power punches. Litzau’s body work and volume were decisive.
Late Rounds: Both fighters tired, but Litzau maintained pressure. The fight was close and competitive — exactly the kind of dogfight that favors the hungrier, more aggressive man on the scorecards.
Outcome: Two judges favored Litzau’s activity and effective aggression. The third saw it for Caballero’s cleaner boxing.

Punch Stats (CompuBox):

Litzau was busier and did more visible damage.
Caballero landed more power shots in some metrics, but Litzau’s output and ring generalship won the night.

Why Litzau Won (Upset Factors)

Superior Will & Pressure — Litzau refused to be intimidated and made the fight ugly for the taller southpaw.
Effective Aggression — Judges rewarded the fighter who forced the action and landed the harder, more noticeable shots.
Preparation — Litzau came in with a clear game plan to close distance and work inside, neutralizing Caballero’s reach.
Heart of a Minnesota Fighter — Classic underdog story: hometown pride, never-say-die attitude.

Aftermath & Legacy

The win was huge for Litzau’s career — it put him in the world title picture and earned massive respect.
A rematch was discussed but never materialized at the top level.
Litzau later fought Adrien Broner (controversial KO loss in 2012) and continued until around 2016.
This fight remains a highlight reel example of activity beating pedigree when the underdog brings elite pressure.

